  "question": {
    "text": "The radius of the smallest circle which touches the parabolas $y=x^2+2$ and $x=y^2+2$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "v5n7W",
      "text": "$\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "LoBHL",
      "text": "$\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{16}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "hVHdy",
      "text": "$\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{4}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "jtElJ",
      "text": "$\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{8}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "The given parabolas are symmetric about the line $y=x$<br/><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/DFGMFxnnXiuvYPhT62tZtj9juligsHozKB46Y0fNXbw.original.fullsize.png\"/><br/>Tangents at $\\mathrm{A} \\& \\mathrm{~B}$ must be parallel to $\\mathrm{y}=\\mathrm{x}$ line, so slope of the tangents $=1$<br/>$\\left(\\frac{d y}{d x}\\right)_{\\min A}=1=\\left(\\frac{d y}{d x}\\right)_{\\min B}$<br/>For point $\\mathrm{B}, \\mathrm{y}=\\mathrm{x}^2+2$<br/>$\\begin{gathered}<br/>\\frac{d y}{d x}=2 x=1 \\\\\nx=\\frac{1}{2} \\Rightarrow y=\\frac{9}{4} \\\\\n\\therefore \\text { Point } B=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\Rightarrow \\text { Point } \\mathrm{A}=\\left(\\frac{9}{4}, \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\\n\\mathrm{AB}=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{9}{4}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2} \\\\\n=\\sqrt{\\frac{98}{16}}=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{4}<br/>\\end{gathered}$<br/>Radius $==\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{8}$",
    "image": null
  },
